$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The incident and the attributed role</h2>
<p>The FIR, registered on 27 December 2020, describes a firing the previous evening in Trilokpuri in which one person was killed and fourteen were injured. The fatal shot is attributed to a co-accused. The applicant&rsquo;s attributed role was loading weapons during the incident; no weapon was recovered from him. He did not join the investigation after a notice of 5 January 2021, proceedings under Sections 82 and 83 CrPC followed, and he surrendered before the Magistrate on 7 July 2021. Barring interim bail spells, he has been in custody since.</p>

<h2>How a successive application is supposed to be built</h2>
<p>Successive bail applications fail when they simply repeat the earlier ones. They succeed when the record has moved. Here, the earlier applications had been withdrawn or not pressed against the backdrop that the injured witnesses were yet to be examined — the classic apprehension being witness influence. By the time this application was heard, the State&rsquo;s own reply of 25 August 2026 confirmed that all the injured witnesses had been examined, with 19 of 60 prosecution witnesses recorded overall. The very contingency that had stood in the way of bail had passed.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Stage reached.</strong> All injured witnesses examined — the tampering apprehension at its most acute had been exhausted.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Parity.</strong> Co-accused Salman, attributed the identical weapon-loading role, had been on bail since 4 January 2024.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Custody and delay.</strong> More than five years inside, 41 witnesses still to be examined, and no realistic prospect of early conclusion — the Court invoked the principles recognised in <em>Union of India v. K.A. Najeeb</em>, (2021) 3 SCC 713.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Conduct on interim bail.</strong> The applicant had surrendered on time after his interim bail spells — evidence that liberty had not been abused.</div>
</div>

<h2>Takeaways for bail practice</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Practice point</th><th>Why it mattered here</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Withdraw with liberty, renew at the defined stage</td><td>The application was heard on merits precisely because the earlier withdrawals had marked out the stage for renewal</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Use the State&rsquo;s status report</td><td>The prosecution&rsquo;s own figures — 19 of 60 witnesses, all injured examined — did the heavy lifting</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Plead parity with precision</td><td>Identity of attributed role with a co-accused on bail, not mere co-accused status, is what parity requires</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Document conduct on interim bail</td><td>Timely surrender after interim bail is concrete proof against flight risk</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note">This report summarises an order of the High Court of Delhi dated 26 November 2026 for general information. It is not legal advice, and it expresses no view on the pending trial.</div>
